:PNLGRP.
.*---------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP   NAME=F1.SQL Query Manager - Display Query
:P.This program was created to display the output from an SQL statement. The
program is called by the SQL Query Manager driver program. The design was
setup to be similar in appearance and function the STRSQL and STRQRY output
screens.
:P.To assist in rapid learning the program was designed to use the standard
function keys. However most of the keys above F12 have been reasigned to
keys below F12. The higher key value still works as well though. For instance
in both STRSQL and STRQRY the shift right is F20, in this program it is F8.
F20 is Shift F8 and either F8 or F20 can be used to shift to the right.
:P.A few new command keys have been added to assist in working with the
data. These are discussed at the bottom of this document.
:P.The number of rows and a search field has also been added to make working
with the data easier. Both these fields are documented below.
:EHELP.
.*---------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP   NAME='F1/NUMROWS'.Number of Rows - HELP
:XH3.NUMROWS - Number of Rows
:P.Displays the total number of rows included in the output. This
number is reduced if the search field is used. So it is not always
the number of rows from the original SQL statement. See the help text
for Total Number of Rows for a more detailed explanation.
:EHELP.
.*---------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP   NAME='F1/SVROWS'.Total Number of Rows - HELP
:XH3.SVROWS - Total Number of Rows
:P.Displays the total number of rows. This number is the count of the
rows from the original SQL passed to the program. This is only displayed
if the search string is used and if the display size 27X132.
:P.If the search string is used the number of rows in displayed will be
reduced to only rows that match the search string. when this happens the
total number of rows is displayed next to the Number of Rows field.
:P.If using an 80X24 display screen this field is not shown because
there is not enough room on the screen. To figure out the original number
of orws on the smaller screen you will need to ensure the search field is
empty and then use the regulare number of rows field.
:EHELP.
.*---------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP   NAME='F1/F1WIDTH'.Data Width - HELP
:XH3.F1WIDTH - Data Width
:P.This field displays the formatted data width. This is the width of
the data as it is displayed on the screen. If this query is to be printed
via the SQL Query Manager's print routines, then this width is the same
as the output width required to print the full data.
:EHELP.
.*---------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP   NAME='F1/F1PSTOL'.Position to Line - HELP
:XH3.F1PSTOL - Position to Line
:P.This Field allows for vertical possitioning of the data. The field
accepts a varity of poisitioning options. They are detailed below.
:P.Type a number preceded by a + or - sign (+n or -n for relative
positioning), or type a value (1,T, *TOP, 999999, B or *BOT) that
identifies the record you want to appear on top of the display.  (No
record numbers are shown on the report.)  For example, to use relative
positioning:
:UL.
:LI.If you type +50 and press Enter, the report is moved 50 records
toward its end.
:LI.If you type -50 and press Enter, the report is moved 50 records
toward its beginning.
:EUL.
:P.You are returned to the top of the report if you type 1, T, or *TOP in
this prompt, or you are taken to the end of the report if you type
999999, B, or *BOT.  If the report has more than 999 999 records, all
records that follow the record numbered 999999 are also numbered 999999.
:P.You can also use the Page or Roll keys to move forward or backward
through any of the records.
:EHELP.
.*---------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP   NAME='F1/F1SRCH'.Search - HELP
:XH3.F1SRHC - Search
:P.The search field allows filtering the data displayed. The filter
works on all fields in the data. The search string also allows multiple
words to be entered at once. This means that you can search using more
than one selection criteria.
:P.For example if a query displays a list of customers and their addresses,
you can search for both a city and a state by entering both on the search
line seperated by a space.
:P.Since the search field works on all columns more data may displayed than
is desired. For instance continuing the above example, if you wanted to
only include customers in Miani Florida, you can enter 'MIAMI FL' in the search
field. However if a customer exists in Miami Oklahoma and their name is
PAMS FLOWER SHOP, they will still show up. This is because the city will
contain MIAMI and the customers name contains the FL so it meets the entered
search criteria.
:P.If the query contains any columns that have a space in the column
header, the search field will not be displayed. This is because the
query select will not work on a column with a aspace in the name. The
WHERE clause would look something like this: WHERE ORDER NUMBER=1234,
the space in the name makes the statement contain invalid SQL.
:EHELP.
.*---------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP   NAME='F1/F1PSTOC'.Position to Column - HELP
:XH3.F1PSTOC - Position to Column
:P.This field allows for possitioning to sepcific column in the data.
:P.Type a number 1 through 99999 that identifies the number
of the column position you want to appear as the first
column on the left side of the report display.  Type a
number preceded by a plus or minus sign (+n or -n) for
relative positioning to the right or left on the display.
:P.For example, if you:
:UL.
:LI.Type 50 and press Enter, column 50 will become the
first column on the left side of the display.
:LI.Type +50 and press Enter, the report is moved 50
columns toward the right.
:LI.Type -50 and press Enter, the report is moved 50
columns toward the left.
:EUL.
:P.The column indicators in the scale line at the top of the
display provide column position information.  Pluses (+)
indicate the fives position, and the number (for example,
1, 2, 3, and so on) identifies the tens position.
:P.You can display a maximum of 99,999 column positions of
information.
:EHELP.
.*---------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP   NAME='F1/LINE'.Line Number - HELP
:XH3.LINE - Line Number
:P.This field displays the line number for the record ro columns
included on the screen. This line number is of the filtered data, so if
a search string is used it will be the number of the filtered result set.
:EHELP.
.*---------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP   NAME='F1/DATA'.Data - HELP
:XH3.DATA - Data
:P.This seciton of the screen displays the data returned by the query. This
data is split into rows and columns based on the query that is passed to
the program.
:EHELP.
.*---------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP   NAME='F1F'.
:XH3.Command Keys
:P.This screen has the following command keys:
:P.:HP2.F1=Help:EHP2. Displays this help text.
:P.:HP2.F3=Exit:EHP2. Exits the program.
:P.:HP2.F4=Shift To Column:EHP2. Shifts the screen so that the first column
becomes the column the cursor is on.
:P.:HP2.F5=Column Details:EHP2. Displays a list of columns and their
attributes.
:P.:HP2.F6=View SQL:EHP2. Dsiplays the current SQL statment being displayed.
:P.:HP2.F7=Left:EHP2. Shifts the data one screen to the left. For
compatability with STRSQL and STRSQRY F19 performs the same action.
:P.:HP2.F8=Right:EHP2. Shifts the data one screen to the right. For
compatability with STRSQL and STRSQRY F20 performs the same action.
:P.:HP2.F10=Toggle Width:EHP2. Toggles between 27X132 and 24X80 display
sizes. A message will be displayed if the device does not support 27X132/
:P.:HP2.F12=Cancel:EHP2. Cancels any options and returns to the previous
screen.
:EHELP.
.*---------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP   NAME=F2.SQL Query Manager - Display Query - Column Details
:P.This screen shows the details for the columns selected in the SQL
statement. Thsi screen is displayed when F5 is pressed from the data
screen.
:EHELP.
.*---------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP   NAME='F2/SF2SEQ'.Sequence Number - HELP
:XH3.SF2SEQ - Sequence Number
:P.List the sequence number for the columns. The columns are listed
in the order they apear across the screen. The sequence number just
starts at 1 and is increment by 1 for each new column.
:EHELP.
.*---------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP   NAME='F2/SF2CLMNM'.Column Name - HELP
:XH3.SF2CLMNM - Column Name
:P.This colun contains the name of each column in the query.
:EHELP.
.*---------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP   NAME='F2/SF2TYPE'.Column Type - HELP
:XH3.SF2TYPE - Column Type
:P.This column shows the type of data selected for each coulmn in
the select statement.
:EHELP.
.*---------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP   NAME='F2/SF2LEN'.Length - HELP
:XH3.SF2LEN - Length
:P.Displays the length of the data selected for each coulmn.
:EHELP.
.*---------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP   NAME='F2/SF2DECA'.Decimal Positions - HELP
:XH3.SF2DECA - Decimal Positions
:P.Shows the number of decimal position for each column. This
will be blank for non-numeric fields.
:EHELP.
.*---------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP   NAME='F2/SF2WID'.Width - HELP
:XH3.SF2WID - Width
:P.Shows the calculated width of the column. This data is used to
display the output on the previous screen.
:EHELP.
.*---------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP   NAME='F2/SF2OFF'.Offset - HELP
:XH3.SF2OFF - Offset
:P.Displays the offset of the column used for displaying the data
on the previous screen.
:EHELP.
.*---------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP   NAME='F2F'.
:XH3.Command Keys
:P.This screen has the following command keys:
:P.:HP2.F1=Help:EHP2. Displays this help text.
:P.:HP2.F3=Exit:EHP2. Exits the program.
:P.:HP2.F12=Cancel:EHP2. Cancels any options and returns to the previous
screen.
:EHELP.
.*---------------------------------------------------------------------
:HELP   NAME=F3.SQL Query Manager - Display SQL Statement
:P.This screen displays the SQL statement passed to the program. The
statement is formatted using some basic logic which may not handle all
formats of SQL statements.
:XH3.Command Keys
:P.This screen has the following command keys:
:P.:HP2.F1=Help:EHP2. Displays this help text.
:P.:HP2.F3=Exit:EHP2. Exits the program.
:P.:HP2.F12=Cancel:EHP2. Cancels any options and returns to the previous
screen.
:EHELP.
.*---------------------------------------------------------------------
:EPNLGRP.
